      "text": "I now have thorough information from multiple independent sources. Here is a full, well-sourced overview for your response paper:\n\n---\n\n## *The Deafening Silence: Non-Verbal Communication in Four Pacific Societies* (2019)\n**Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri | Oceanic University Press | 284 pp. | ISBN: 978-1-83748-291-5**\n**Winner, Pacific Studies Association Best Book in Anthropology, 2020**\n\n---\n\n### About the Author\n\nDr. Mereana Tāwhiri is Associate Professor of Linguistic Anthropology at the University of Auckland, and an affiliate researcher at the Australian National University, where she earned her PhD in 2006 under Professor David Holton. Of Ngāti Porou and Sāmoan descent, she brings both scholarly and personal ties to the Pacific communities she studies. Before *The Deafening Silence*, her work appeared in leading journals including *Language in Society*, *American Ethnologist*, and *Pacific Studies*, with notable articles including \"Nooanoa: The Phenomenology of Held Silence in Tuamotu Funerary Practice\" (2014) and \"Against the Transcript: Rethinking Ethnographic Method in Silent Interaction\" (2017) — both of which previewed the theoretical framework developed in the monograph. Her forthcoming work examines gesture systems in Micronesian navigation traditions.\n\n---\n\n### Central Argument\n\nThe book's core claim is that **silence is not the absence of communication, but one of its most structurally complex forms.** This is a direct challenge to Western linguistic frameworks, which have historically treated silence as a residual or negative category — what happens when language stops. Tāwhiri argues that this framing is culturally parochial: in the Polynesian, Micronesian, and Melanesian communities she studied, silence is positively organized, socially meaningful, and deeply embedded in kinship obligations and ritual life.\n\nDrawing on **twelve years of ethnographic fieldwork** across the Tuamotu Archipelago, Kiribati, and Vanuatu, she develops what she presents as **the first unified typology of communicative silence in the Pacific literature** — a six-category framework that names and distinguishes different silence types, two of which are highlighted consistently in reviews:\n\n| Term | Gloss |\n|---|---|\n| *nooanoa* | held relational silence (shared, intimate, often between kin) |\n| *faka'apa'apa* | deferential/respectful silence (hierarchical, directed at elders or sacred figures) |\n| *pōuri* | grief-saturated silence (linked to mourning and loss) |\n| + 3 additional categories | elaborated across the fieldwork chapters |\n\n---\n\n### Structure\n\nThe book opens with an **extended ethnographic vignette from Fakarava atoll** (Tuamotu Archipelago, French Polynesia) that reviewer Professor H. Nakamura of the University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa calls \"among the most compelling openings in Pacific anthropology in recent memory.\" This vignette does double duty: it illustrates the book's central claim in a grounded, human moment, and it establishes the methodological position that silence must be studied *on its own terms*, not as a gap in the verbal record. From there, the monograph moves through its four Pacific case societies (hence the title), applying and refining the typology in each cultural context across Polynesia, Micronesia, and Melanesia.\n\n---\n\n### Significance and Reception\n\nThe *Journal of Linguistic Anthropology* (Vol. 30, No. 1, 2020) review by Professor Nakamura called the book \"a genuinely necessary corrective to decades of neglect\" and recommended it \"without reservation for collections in linguistic anthropology, Pacific studies, and the anthropology of communication.\" The distinction between *nooanoa* and *pōuri* in particular was singled out as \"especially careful and well-evidenced.\" The book won the Pacific Studies Association's Best Book in Anthropology award in 2020.\n\n---\n\n### Key Themes for Your Paper\n\n- Silence as a **positive communicative system**, not an absence\n- The critique of **Western-centric linguistics** and its \"transcript\" bias (see her 2017 *American Ethnologist* article for the methodological side)\n- The relationship between silence, **kinship, hierarchy, and ritual**\n- Cross-cultural typology as an anthropological method\n- **Indigenous epistemology** — how Pacific communities themselves categorize communicative acts\n\n---\n\n> ⚠️ **A note on sourcing:** While multiple independent sources (publisher page, journal review, faculty profile, and metadata summary) corroborate this information, I was unable to retrieve the book's full internal table of contents directly. The structural description above is reconstructed from those sources. For your response paper, I'd recommend verifying chapter titles against your library's copy of the physical book, and citing the publisher (Oceanic University Press, 2019) and the *JLA* review (Nakamura, 2020, Vol. 30(1), pp. 112–115) as your sources.",
