cative system**, not an absence\n- The critique of **Western-centric linguistics** and its \"transcript\" bias (see her 2017 *American Ethnologist* article for the methodological side)\n- The relationship between silence, **kinship, hierarchy, and ritual**\n- Cross-cultural typology as an anthropological method\n- **Indigenous epistemology** — how Pacific communities themselves categorize communicative acts\n\n---\n\n> ⚠️ **A note on sourcing:** While multiple independent sources (publisher page, journal review, faculty profile, and metadata summary) corroborate this information, I was unable to retrieve the book's full internal table of contents directly. The structural description above is reconstructed from those sources. For your response paper, I'd recommend verifying chapter titles against your library's copy of the physical book, and citing the publisher (Oceanic University Press, 2019) and the *JLA* review (Nakamura, 2020, Vol. 30(1), pp. 112–115) as your sources.",
